      "i_do_script": "Here the value is fixed and the inequalities change. I substitute the same number into every inequality and decide which ones turn true. Watch: the value is 11. For x < 1, 11 < 1 is false. For x > 10, 11 > 10 is true. For x > -7, 11 > -7 is true. For x < -2, 11 < -2 is false. For x > 14, 11 > 14 is false. The two true inequalities are x > 10 and x > -7.",
      "canonical_error": {
        "pattern": "Student picks an inequality based on which numbers 'look close' to the test value, instead of substituting and comparing — especially confusing > with < when the variable side is read out of order.",
        "example": "For 'select inequalities where 11 is a solution,' the student picks x > 14 because 14 is closest in size to 11, missing that 11 > 14 is false.",
        "why": "The student reads the inequality as a numeric proximity question rather than as a comparison. They see '14' near '11' and assume similarity equals truth, instead of substituting 11 in and checking the direction of the symbol."
      },
      "redirect_script": {
        "stop": "Hold up — substitute first.",
        "prompt": "Write the test number in place of the variable in each inequality, then read the new statement out loud and ask, 'Is this true?' Cross out the ones that turn false.",
        "praise": "Excellent — you let substitution decide, not appearance."
      },
      "vocabulary": [
        {
          "term": "Solution",
          "definition": "A value that makes the inequality true when substituted."
        },
        {
          "term": "Inequality",
          "definition": "A comparison statement using <, >, less-than-or-equal, or greater-than-or-equal."
        }
      ],
      "next_steps": {
