    {
      "skill_id": "6AF2-S6",
      "name": "Use substitution to test combinations in a real-world inequality and justify the choice",
      "column": "looking_forward",
      "parent_standard": "6.AF.2",
      "prerequisite_skill": {
        "type": "gap",
        "grade": 6,
        "description": "Substitute two values for two different variables in a given inequality and decide whether the resulting statement is true.",
        "check_question": "Given 340 + 30a + 45g less-than-or-equal-to 2000, is a = 16, g = 23 a safe load? Show the substitution."
      },
      "i_do_script": "Real-world problems give me a story and a ready-made inequality — I do NOT have to write the inequality, just test combinations in it. Watch: the truck inequality is 340 + 30a + 45g less-than-or-equal-to 2000, and one combination is 16 crates of apples and 23 crates of oranges. I substitute a = 16 and g = 23: 340 + 30(16) + 45(23) = 340 + 480 + 1035 = 1855. Is 1855 at most 2000? Yes — so this combination is safe. For Part B, I write that I substituted 16 and 23 into the inequality, computed 1855, and showed that 1855 is at most 2000.",
      "canonical_error": {
        "pattern": "Student tries to 'solve' the real-world inequality algebraically or write a new inequality from scratch, instead of just substituting each given combination and checking.",
